5️. Project Architecture

The project follows the Page Object Model (POM) design pattern and a simple layered structure:

base — BaseTest (WebDriver setup / teardown, common utilities)

pages — Page classes (locators and page actions: LoginPage, DashboardPage)

tests — Test classes using TestNG (data providers, assertions)

utils — Helpers (Excel reader, screenshot util, logger)

resources — TestData (LoginData.xlsx), config.properties

testng.xml — Suite configuration for grouped execution

This separation improves readability, reusability, and maintainability.

6️. Modules Automated

Login Module — valid credentials positive path

Invalid Login Module — wrong password / empty fields / SQL injection checks (negative tests)

Dashboard Verification Module — assert presence of key UI elements after login

Session Handling Module — verify session persistence and logout behavior

Error Handling & Reporting Module — capture screenshots and logs on failure

Each test is independent and can be executed individually or as part of a suite.

7️. Features of the Project

Implements Page Object Model (POM) for clean page abstraction

Data-driven tests via TestNG @DataProvider (Excel/CSV)

Automatic WebDriver management using WebDriverManager

Screenshot capture for failed tests and logging support

TestNG suite execution (testng.xml) and HTML reports

Maven for dependency management and build lifecycle

Ready for CI integration (GitHub Actions/Jenkins)

8️. Execution Process

Configure config.properties (base URL, browser, timeouts).

Prepare test data (TestData/LoginData.xlsx).

Execute testng.xml (Run as → TestNG Suite).

TestNG initializes WebDriver via BaseTest.

Tests read data from data provider, call page methods, and assert results.

On failure, framework captures screenshot and logs stacktrace.

TestNG generates an execution report (HTML/XML).

Browser closes after each test or suite (based on config).

9️. Expected Outcome

Positive and negative login scenarios execute automatically.

Dashboard and post-login checks pass for valid credentials.

Failures produce screenshots and descriptive error logs for debugging.

TestNG report shows pass/fail stats and test run details.

Framework is modular and easy to extend for other modules.

10. Conclusion

LoginAutomation demonstrates a practical, maintainable approach to automating login validation using Selenium, Java, and TestNG. The project reduces manual testing effort, provides reliable regression checks, and serves as a base that can be extended to automate more workflows (checkout, profile, search) or integrated into CI pipelines for continuous validation.
